--[[
    Fix HD Inventory Icon Position: restore the stack picker on meat and
    patches, dropped by the HD framework's copy of meat_spoiling.script.

    Why this exists:
      This is the sibling of the meat spoiling tooltip fix. The framework's
      meat_spoiling.script is an older fork of ilrathCXV's "Meat Spoiling Timer
      in Tooltips" and wins the load-order conflict over it. The fork drops
      three functions; zzz_aaa_meat_spoiling_tooltip_fix.script restored
      build_desc_footer, and this file restores the other two.

      Anomaly only lets you expand a stack into individual items in the picker
      when the item's section declares use_condition. Meat and patches do not,
      so a stack of them collapses to a single entry and you cannot pick the
      freshest piece -- which is exactly what the spoiling timer exists to let
      you do. ilrathCXV worked around it by swapping ui_inventory's SYS_GetParam
      for the duration of Picker_Toggle, so that use_condition reads as true for
      meat and patches only, then putting the original back.

      ui_inventory.script calls SYS_GetParam unqualified, so it resolves through
      the ui_inventory namespace table before falling back to _G. Assigning
      ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam therefore shadows the global for that one module
      and nothing else, and only for the duration of the call.

      Delegation matters as much as the swap. Another mod may already own
      ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam, and its answers have to survive our detour.
      G.A.M.M.A. Artefacts Reinvention installs exactly such a shim at chunk
      load (zz_item_artefact.script, gated on MCM enable_condition_bar, default
      on): it reports use_condition as true for every degradable artefact kind,
      which is what gives artefacts their condition bar and their picker. It
      writes into ui_inventory and utils_ui only, never _G, so a shim of ours
      that falls back to the global SYS_GetParam instead of to the displaced
      function makes artefacts read use_condition = false for the duration of
      Picker_Toggle. The bar still draws (utils_ui is untouched) but the stack
      refuses to expand. v0.6.0 and v0.7.0 shipped the non-chaining version and
      broke artefact stacks that way; the shim now chains.

    Credit for the original approach: ilrathCXV, via Utjan's "Tagged Patches".

    Deviations from ilrathCXV's version, both deliberate:
      - the previous value of ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam is saved and restored
        rather than cleared to nil, so we cannot stomp another mod's override
      - Picker_Toggle's return value is passed through rather than swallowed

    Load order: the zzz_ prefix puts our on_game_start after meat_spoiling's, so
    meat_spoiling.meats is populated and Picker_Toggle is whatever the mod chain
    settled on by then.
--]]



-- new_get_param exists only in ilrathCXV's copy. If it is present then that
-- copy won the load order, this behaviour is already installed, and we must not
-- add a second layer of it.
if meat_spoiling and meat_spoiling.new_get_param then
    return
end

-- meats is a global in both copies; without it we have nothing to test against.
if not (meat_spoiling and meat_spoiling.meats) then
    return
end

if not (ui_inventory and ui_inventory.UIInventory and ui_inventory.UIInventory.Picker_Toggle) then
    return
end



local meats = meat_spoiling.meats

-- Whatever ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam was when we displaced it. Held in an
-- upvalue rather than closed over per call so the shim is allocated once; it is
-- saved and restored around the call exactly like the swap itself, so a nested
-- Picker_Toggle cannot leave the wrong delegate behind.
local delegate = nil

-- Report use_condition as true for meat and patches, delegate everything else.
local function picker_get_param(typ, sec, param, def)
    if (param == "use_condition") and sec then
        if meats[sec] or (tagged_patches and tagged_patches.is_patch(nil, sec)) then
            return true
        end
    end
    return (delegate or SYS_GetParam)(typ, sec, param, def)
end

function on_game_start()
    local base_Picker_Toggle = ui_inventory.UIInventory.Picker_Toggle

    ui_inventory.UIInventory.Picker_Toggle = function(self, bag, idx, update_mode, force_hide)
        local prev = ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam
        local prev_delegate = delegate

        -- Never chain to ourselves: if we are already installed (a nested call
        -- got here first) keep pointing at the mod underneath us. Written long
        -- rather than as `(prev ~= picker_get_param) and prev or prev_delegate`,
        -- which collapses a nil prev to prev_delegate and so would resurrect an
        -- override that intervening code had deliberately cleared.
        if prev == picker_get_param then
            delegate = prev_delegate
        else
            delegate = prev
        end
        ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam = picker_get_param

        -- pcall so an error anywhere in the picker path cannot leave our shim
        -- installed for the rest of the session; the file's containment claim
        -- is only true if the restore is unconditional. The error is re-raised
        -- unchanged, so this hides nothing.
        local ok, result = pcall(base_Picker_Toggle, self, bag, idx, update_mode, force_hide)

        ui_inventory.SYS_GetParam = prev
        delegate = prev_delegate

        if not ok then
            error(result, 0)
        end
        return result
    end
end
